<!DOCTYPE html>
<html   lang="en">
    <title>
        Leraning React
    </title>
    <body>        
        <div id="root"></div>
        <script crossorigin src="https://unpkg.com/react@18/umd/react.development.js"></script>
        <script crossorigin src="https://unpkg.com/react-dom@18/umd/react-dom.development.js"></script>
        <script>
            let name    = "Rohan";        
            const rootEle =  document.getElementById("root");
            setInterval(() => {
                name = name === "Rohan" ? "Mike" : "Rohan";
            }, 5000);
            const element = React.createElement(
                "h1",
                null,
                "Hi,My name is "+name
            )
            const root = ReactDOM.createRoot(rootEle);
            root.render(element);
        </script>
    </body>
</html>
code is valid, and React will render:

Hi, My name is Rohan

But after 5 seconds, when name changes to "Mike", the UI will still say Rohan.

Let's understand exactly why.

What happens step by step
Initially:

let name = "Rohan";

So:

name
 ↓
"Rohan"

Then you create the React element:

const element = React.createElement(
    "h1",
    null,
    "Hi, My name is " + name
);

At this exact moment, JavaScript evaluates:

"Hi,My name is " + name

which becomes:

"Hi,My name is Rohan"

So your React element is effectively describing:

<h1>
    Hi,My name is Rohan
</h1>

Then:

const root = ReactDOM.createRoot(rootEle);
root.render(element);

React renders it:

Hi, My name is Rohan

Then the interval runs
After 5 seconds:

name = name === "Rohan" ? "Mike" : "Rohan";

Now:

name
 ↓
"Mike"

But here's the important part:

Nothing happened to element.

Your existing element still represents:

<h1>
    Hi, My name is Rohan
</h1>

It doesn't magically become:

<h1>
    Hi, My name is Mike
</h1>

And you don't call:

root.render(...)

again either.

So:

JavaScript variable
        ↓
      changes
        ↓
      "Mike"

        X

React element
        ↓
 still says "Rohan"

        ↓
      DOM
        ↓
 still says "Rohan"

Reason: This happens because React does not treat every JavaScript variable as state.We have to explicitly tell React:
"This value is part of the component's state."
Then, when that state changes, React can re-render the component and update the UI accordingly.
